00:00:01.439 --> 00:00:03.189 align:start position:0%
 
welcome back to the omics logic  bioinformatics for precision medicine  program in this video we will be  reviewing what we have learned in  session three the third session on  multiple sequence alignment and  phylogenetic analysis is designed to  build upon the genomic data analysis  skills that you have learned in previous  session  so if you have not gone through the  previous session make sure to complete  the associated coursework and example  projects before proceeding with session  three  to briefly review what we have learned  when we compare multiple genomes from  different samples of patients there are  a couple of questions that comes to our  mind are these genomes different how  different are they which portions of the  genome are more variable which portions  of the genomes are similar to understand  whether genomes are different we can  align them to a reference known as  pairwise alignment or compare multiple  genomes to each other using multiple  sequence alignment  next we learned about percentage  distance or similarity matrix to  quantify the differences in sequences of  sarcop2 and other coronal virus strains  we can generate the similarity or  distance matrix here as we can see in  the image the distance matrix clearly  shows humans are cov2 and the pangolin  coronary virus are more similar whereas  sar cov2 and have nearly 70 to 79  percent similarity with bad coronary  virus  at this point you might be wondering  whether all mutations at nucleotide  level are carried out at amino acid  level or not so here we can take a look  at multiple sequence alignment  representing the differences in the  sequences  at amino acid level  here we can mainly see three things  first there are seven to nine persons  dissimilarity between human sarco v2 and  the pangolin corona virus while 15 to 17  percent dissimilarity is observed  between human psycho2 and the back  corner virus  we can also see that not all variations  at nucleotide level get transferred at  amino acid level as there is a higher  percentage of variations at nucleotide  level than at amino acid level besides  we can also see that similar to the  trend at the nucleotide level variation  sarco v2 is more similar to the pangolin  coronavirus than the bad source and bad  coronavirus  but what can we achieve by comparing the  genomics data of pathogens from patients  we can understand the composition of  viral hypertypes in a given patient  sample  now how do we achieve this we can use a  computational technique known as short  red assembly into haplotypes that is  used to measure genetic diversity among  diverse samples  so far we learned about multiple  sequence alignment next we will learn  how closely related are these genomes  that multiple genomes or whole groups of  genome originate from the same parent  genome or not to find answers to this  question we can perform phylogenetic  analysis to understand the relationship  among them and their origin  here we learnt how analysis of  phylogenetic trees allows for  conclusions to be drawn about epidemic  and pandemic viruses the sequences and  their data can be used to estimate viral  emergence characterize geographic spread  of the virus reconstruct epidemiological  dynamics of viral spread and identify  instances of the adaptation  next we learned about building the  phylogenetic analysis pipeline for  multiple sequence alignment on the  t-biome for server the pipeline  consisted of several steps and each of  these steps are discussed in detail  during the session to summarize we begin  we begin by using start option for  multiple sequence alignment of the  sequences we use three algorithms  multiple sequence alignment for the  nucleotide sequences multiple sequence  alignment a a to incorporate the changes  at the amino acid level and select codon  position multiple sequence alignment to  determine the codon precisions for the  phylogenetic analysis finally we can  generate the phylogenetic tree from  multiple sequence alignment using the  beast speciation work that process  algorithm  afterwards click on the end  button and run the pipeline using a  suitable name  as mentioned in this session the basic  output from the pipeline is a  phylogenetic tree based on multiple  sequence alignment in the dendrogram we  find that the samples are arranged into  two main branches earlier sequences from  the cap on and later samples from  liberia and great britain in the output  file full table dot tsp contains the  details of the sequences that are  aligned at the amino acid level and the  nucleotide level hence the multiple  sequence alignment algorithm gives us  the relation of both these changes  then we discuss some of the key terms  associated with variance such as  variance of interest variance concern  and variance of high consequences and  how these terms differ from each other  we also discussed about the prevalence  of variance contained in the d614g  mutation which has become the wild type  form of sarcasev2 worldwide since mid  2020. we also learned that variants can  become extinct due to natural selection  for instance we discussed about in june  2020 a concerning sarsio v2 variant  thought to present a decreased  sensitivity to the immune response was  detected in mint in the netherlands and  denmark however this variant was  declared extinct just a few months later  and is no longer in circulation  we also discussed about the current  three variants of concern which are  named based on the country from where  they were first identified the alpha  beta and gamma the most concerning is  how these mutations will affect efficacy  of vaccines or protection they offer but  they are also a reflection of how  viruses evolve under different immune  pressures then we discussed about the  increasing prevalence of drug resistant  forms of tuberculosis infection  requiring more effective treatment  strategies here we learned about drug  resistance in turboclasses various  groups of tuberculosis drugs mechanism  of action and genes resistant to these  drugs antibiotics and their mechanisms  of action  the treatment of drug resistant  tuberculosis was more complicated and  can take up to 18 months depending upon  the resistance profile of the infection  as we can see here over the course of 21  years one can observe the changes in the  treatment option that were required to  keep the pathogen under control hence  the variant profiling of the pathogen is  absolutely necessary to manage the  tuberculosis infection  for this we took an example of running  the mutation variant calling pipeline on  the t-bin for server using the mutect2  pipeline what does the algorithm do and  how to interpret the results that also  cover in the session  the contents of session 3 correspond  with practical lessons that you can  complete independently available on the  omixlogic learn portal for this session  we have covered concepts based on  lessons from first 3 genomics the course  serves as an introduction to the  bioinformatics sub-discipline of  genomics where students will be  familiarized with the biology of  genetics and genetic variation while  considering path practical application  within the  course specific lessons that are of the  relevance include lesson 6 phylogenetic  analysis where you will learn about  phylogenetic analysis pipeline building  analysis and biological interpretation  for sarco v2 zoonotic origin  lesson 7 comparing dna sequences using  phylogenetic analysis  will teach you about the generation  about phylogenetic tree from the dna  sequences of samples to understand  evolutionary relationships among them  next we have lesson 8 multiple sequence  alignment visualizing and filtering  alignment here you will learn about how  you can perform and visualize multiple  sequence alignment and filter out the  varying information  the full course contains a lot more  information that you can choose to  explore independently at your own pace  so do not worry about completing all of  them immediately  another great resource that you will  have access to is project 11  surveillance of sar cov2 variance of  concern in this project you will conduct  surveillance of sarco v2 variants of  concern from wastewater samples by  downloading data from the database  perform multiple sequence alignment and  phylogenetic analysis and derive  biological significance and understand  the evolutionary relationship between  samples  another great resource is project 17  tuberculosis infection and treatment  here you will learn to extract data from  big data repositories for patients  infected with mycobacterium tuberculosis  and run pipeline on the t by info  platform to get hands-on experience with  mutation variant calling interpret  biological insights by searching for the  protein variants in the patient's  isolate over 21 years of infection and  look for specific mutations belonging to  each isolate responsible for drug  resistance  in the upcoming session 4 on  transactional data analysis you will  learn what is rna sequencing how do you  prepare your data for rna seq data  analysis how do you carry out biological  implementation of the outputs how is  machine learning used in transatlantic  data analysis and at the end of session  4 you will have a thorough understanding  of transatomic data and be able to carry  out analysis of cancer datasets using  bioinformatic pipelines and various  machine learning techniques

